      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">write_jsonl</data>
      <data key="d11">write_jsonl</data>
      <data key="d12">function</data>
      <data key="d3">repo2graph/export.py</data>
      <data key="d5">python</data>
      <data key="d13">104</data>
      <data key="d14">116</data>
      <data key="d15">def write_jsonl(path: Path, rows) -&gt; int:</data>
      <data key="d16">Stream `rows` to `path` as JSONL; return how many were written.

    newline="\n" (ISS-28) + atomic: a crash mid-write must not leave a truncated
    last line that read_jsonl's json.loads then dies on. Streaming means `rows`
    may be a generator (build_chunks) that is never fully materialised.</data>
      <data key="d4">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="3734.30" y="694.40" width="111.60" height="31.20" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">write_jsonl</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
    <node id="sym:repo2graph/export.py::_grid_pairs">
      <data key="d0">sym:repo2graph/export.py::_grid_pairs</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">_grid_pairs</data>
      <data key="d11">_grid_pairs</data>
      <data key="d12">function</data>
      <data key="d3">repo2graph/export.py</data>
      <data key="d5">python</data>
      <data key="d13">133</data>
      <data key="d14">149</data>
      <data key="d15">def _grid_pairs(pos, cell):</data>
      <data key="d16">Yield the node pairs sitting within one grid cell of each other.

    Every pair lands in the same bucket or in two adjacent ones, and each
    unordered pair is yielded once: only four of the eight neighbouring cells
    are scanned, the other four see the pair from their own side.</data>
      <data key="d4">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="3869.90" y="694.40" width="116.25" height="32.50" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">_grid_pairs</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
    <node id="sym:repo2graph/export.py::_spring">
      <data key="d0">sym:repo2graph/export.py::_spring</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">_spring</data>
      <data key="d11">_spring</data>
      <data key="d12">function</data>
      <data key="d3">repo2graph/export.py</data>
      <data key="d5">python</data>
      <data key="d13">152</data>
      <data key="d14">197</data>
      <data key="d15">def _spring(nodes, adjacency, iterations):</data>
      <data key="d16">Fruchterman-Reingold in pure Python: networkx's needs numpy, we do not.

    Repulsion runs only between nodes less than 2k apart, bucketed on a grid.
    Past that distance the k^2/d term moves a node by a rounding error, while
    the all-pairs form costs O(n^2) per iteration and dominates big exports.</data>
      <data key="d4">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="4010.15" y="694.40" width="82.88" height="33.15" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">_spring</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
